y_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":13,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"reviewer\":\"numerical_auditor\",\"paper_id\":\"bicycle-infrastructure-paper\",\"run_status\":\"ok\",\"summary\":\"Most reported counts, percentages and shares reconcile. These include the 70/36/34 pilot funnel, 49% and 15% retained, the 5+8+21 coding split, Tables 3 and 4, Table 6's date-precision sum to 43, 18+26=44 and 17+2=19 day-dated projects, Table 8's 266,625+433,954=700,579 and every share, and the figure and text values for Figures 2 to 4. Four material numerical defects remain. (1) The headline pilot yield of \\\"2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent)\\\" divides distinct openings by articles. The article-level count is 5 coded relevant (15%) or 3 after recoding, and Figure 1's caption makes the same openings-over-hits mix. (2) Footnote 3 says the Stord article was coded not relevant by both human coders, but Table 3 and the Section 4.1 text imply the second coder coded it relevant. (3) The Q1 2024 gap in Figure 3 is described as a missing quarter, while the appendix says January to April 2024 is missing. (4) The Table 9 first-type rule contradicts its own example, which affects the category shares in Table 8 and Figure 2. Parser-quality warnings were respected: figures and tables were checked against page text and images, and the structured table CSV/markdown was not used for cell assignment.\",\"findings\":[{\"id\":\"NUM-001\",\"category\":\"unit_denominator_mismatch\",\"finding_summary\":\"The pilot yield \\\"Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place\\\" divides 2 distinct openings by 34 articles. At article level the paper itself reports 5 articles coded relevant (15 % of 34), or 3 after recoding.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"medium\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"location\":{\"page\":4,\"page_label\":\"4\",\"section\":\"3.1.2 Pilot sample / Figure 1\",\"text_quote\":\"Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place.\",\"precision\":\"exact\"},\"claim_text\":\"Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place. Figure 1 caption: Only 2 of 70 search hits concern a cycling infrastructure opening that actually took place.\",\"assessment\":\"partially\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"The numerator '2' is the count of distinct openings (Homborsund and Stod/Steinkjer), and the denominator is articles (34) or search hits (70). Figure 1 shows 5 unique articles coded relevant (15 % retained) and 2 distinct openings. Section 3.1.4 says there are five relevant articles but only two unique openings (four on Homborsund, one on Stod), and that two Homborsund articles were published before the opening, leaving three relevant articles. Table 2 also lists 4 articles for Homborsund. 2/34 = 5.9 %, which matches the printed 'about 6 percent' arithmetically, but the share of articles concerning a realised opening is 5/34 = 14.7 % (Figure 1's 15 %), or 3/34 = 8.8 % under the validation recoding. The statement therefore mixes units (events over articles), and it understates the article-level yield. The abstract's 'only two actual openings' counts events and is not affected. The same openings-over-hits mix appears in the Figure 1 caption ('2 of 70 search hits'). The caption's own 70 hits include duplicates, so the number of hits tied to these two openings is not reported.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 4 text (Section 3.1.2 and Figure 1)\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_004.md\",\"page\":4,\"page_label\":\"4\",\"section\":\"3.1.2\",\"text_quote\":\"Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place.\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-1a119a3cb70f2153fc56dd97\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Figure 1 chart values (70; 34, 49 % retained; 5, 15 % retained; 2) confirmed on page_images/page_004.png, since the figure_1 crop omits the chart.\"},{\"id\":\"S2\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 4 text, Figure 1 labels\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_004.md\",\"page\":4,\"page_label\":\"4\",\"section\":\"Figure 1\",\"text_quote\":\"Coded “relevant” 5 15 % retained\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-1a119a3cb70f2153fc56dd97\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Page text matches the chart seen in the page image.\"},{\"id\":\"S3\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"full_text.md, Section 3.1.4\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/full_text.md\",\"page\":5,\"page_label\":\"5\",\"section\":\"3.1.4 Manual coding of the pilot\",\"text_quote\":\"Of the five relevant articles, only two unique openings were found.\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-a50e96cda8f251a4eabe3e71\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Section 3.1.4 sits on page 5, and the registered object covers the whole full_text.md.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"2 of 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\",\"S2\",\"S3\"],\"relation\":\"partial\",\"note\":\"2 is a count of openings, not of articles. The article-level counts in the paper are 5 (15 %) or 3.\"}],\"numeric_check\":{\"kind\":\"cross_source\",\"quantities\":[{\"value\":\"6\",\"source_id\":\"S1\",\"unit\":\"percent\",\"sample\":\"34 unique pilot articles\",\"denominator\":\"34 unique articles\",\"context\":\"printed share, numerator is 2 distinct openings, not articles\",\"rounding_half_width\":\"0.5\",\"scale\":\"percent\"},{\"value\":\"15\",\"source_id\":\"S2\",\"unit\":\"percent\",\"sample\":\"34 unique pilot articles\",\"denominator\":\"34 unique articles\",\"context\":\"Figure 1: share of unique articles coded 'relevant' (5 of 34 = 14.7 %)\",\"rounding_half_width\":\"0.5\",\"scale\":\"percent\"},{\"value\":\"2\",\"source_id\":\"S3\",\"unit\":\"distinct openings\",\"sample\":\"5 articles coded relevant in the pilot\",\"denominator\":\"not applicable (count)\",\"context\":\"Section 3.1.4: five relevant articles describe only two unique openings\",\"rounding_half_width\":\"0.5\",\"scale\":\"number\"}],\"comparability\":\"The 6 % and the 15 % both use the 34 unique pilot articles as the denominator, but the numerators count different units: 2 distinct openings versus 5 articles coded relevant (3 after the pre-opening Homborsund articles are recoded). They are not the same estimand, and the 6 % is labelled as an article share.\",\"observed_discrepancy\":\"The article-level share that the paper itself reports is 5/34 = 14.7 % (Figure 1 shows 15 %), or 3/34 = 8.8 % after recoding. The text reports 2/34 = 5.9 % as an article share.\",\"possible_explanation\":\"The authors may have meant the share of unique openings per article, or may have intended to say 2 openings from 34 articles. The numerator unit was not carried through.\",\"suggested_correction\":\"State the unit explicitly. Either report the article-level yield (5 of 34, 15 %, or 3 of 34 after recoding) or phrase it as 'the 34 articles describe only 2 distinct openings'. Apply the same to the Figure 1 caption's '2 of 70 search hits'.\"},\"suggested_fix\":\"Rewrite the sentence and the Figure 1 caption so the numerator and denominator use the same unit (articles with articles, or events with events), and reconcile the 6 % with the 15 % shown in Figure 1.\"},{\"id\":\"NUM-002\",\"category\":\"cross_source_count_inconsistency\",\"finding_summary\":\"Footnote 3 says the Stord project was coded not relevant by both human coders, but Table 3 and Section 4.1 show the second coder coded the one disputed article, the Stord article, as relevant.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"medium\",\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"location\":{\"page\":10,\"page_label\":\"10\",\"section\":\"6 Further Uses of the Articles, footnote 3\",\"text_quote\":\"the project in Stord, which appears only in the pilot and was coded as not\",\"precision\":\"exact\"},\"claim_text\":\"The 44 projects include the 43 in Section 5 and the project in Stord, which appears only in the pilot and was coded as not relevant by both human coders (Section 4).\",\"assessment\":\"no\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"Table 3 gives the pilot coder Relevant = 3 and the second coder Relevant = 4. The only off-diagonal cell is pilot Not relevant / second Relevant = 1, so the second coder coded one extra article as relevant. Section 4.1 says the single disagreement 'covers a dispute between Stord municipality and Vestland County', and Section 4.2 says the same Stord article is the only pilot coder versus LLM disagreement. Footnote 3 instead states that both human coders coded the Stord project as not relevant. Both statements cannot hold for the same article. The pilot-article tally supports the Table 3 and 4 reading: the footnote's 'four from the pilot run' (96 = 92 + 4) matches the 4 LLM-relevant articles in Table 4 (the 3 agreed articles plus Stord). If the Stord project and the disputed Stord article are distinct, the paper does not say so. The composition of the 4 pilot articles and the overlap of the pilot with rounds 2 to 4 are not documented, so the 96 and 44 totals cannot be rebuilt from retained evidence.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 10 text, footnote 3\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_010.md\",\"page\":10,\"page_label\":\"10\",\"section\":\"6 footnote 3\",\"text_quote\":\"the project in Stord, which appears only in the pilot and was coded as not\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-e9b90e0f75ce05ff1ce150cc\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Footnote text is split across lines in the page text; the quoted fragment is verbatim.\"},{\"id\":\"S2\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 7 text, Section 4.1\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_007.md\",\"page\":7,\"page_label\":\"7\",\"section\":\"4.1\",\"text_quote\":\"The only article they disagree on covers a dispute between Stord municipality and Vestland County over a pedestrian crossing.\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-4e6da301f188ae5c7e5d0a63\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Table 3 cells (3, 0, 3; 1, 30, 31; 4, 30, 34) agree between the page text and the parser-quality audit's comparison with the table image.\"},{\"id\":\"S3\",\"type\":\"table\",\"label\":\"Table 3 markdown (body cells)\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/tables/table_3.md\",\"page\":7,\"page_label\":\"7\",\"section\":\"Table 3\",\"text_quote\":\"Overall agreement 97% (33 of 34 articles)\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-e3774f186493fe1cf3afe556\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Header cells in the markdown are merged, so the second-coder total of 4 was read from page 7 text and the table image.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"Stord project coded not relevant by both human coders\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\",\"S2\",\"S3\"],\"relation\":\"contradicts\",\"note\":\"Table 3 shows the second coder with 4 relevant versus 3 for the pilot coder, and the disputed article is the Stord article.\"}],\"numeric_check\":{\"kind\":\"cross_source\",\"quantities\":[{\"value\":\"4\",\"source_id\":\"S3\",\"unit\":\"articles\",\"sample\":\"34 unique pilot articles\",\"denominator\":\"not applicable (count)\",\"context\":\"Table 3: second coder 'Relevant' total\",\"rounding_half_width\":\"0.5\",\"scale\":\"number\"},{\"value\":\"3\",\"source_id\":\"S3\",\"unit\":\"articles\",\"sample\":\"34 unique pilot articles\",\"denominator\":\"not applicable (count)\",\"context\":\"Table 3: pilot coder 'Relevant' total\",\"rounding_half_width\":\"0.5\",\"scale\":\"number\"},{\"value\":\"1\",\"source_id\":\"S2\",\"unit\":\"articles\",\"sample\":\"34 unique pilot articles\",\"denominator\":\"not applicable (count)\",\"context\":\"the only article on which the two human coders disagree is the Stord article\",\"rounding_half_width\":\"0.5\",\"scale\":\"number\"}],\"comparability\":\"The same 34 pilot articles and the same relevance coding are used in Table 3, Section 4.1 and footnote 3. The footnote's 'project in Stord' may refer to a different article from the disputed 'Stord municipality and Vestland County' article, which the paper does not clarify.\",\"observed_discrepancy\":\"If the Stord project is the disputed article, the second coder coded it relevant (Table 3 total 4 vs 3, one off-diagonal article), which contradicts footnote 3's 'not relevant by both human coders'.\",\"possible_explanation\":\"The footnote may refer to a different Stord project article, or may describe an earlier coding version.\",\"suggested_correction\":\"Reconcile footnote 3 with Table 3 and Section 4.1, state explicitly whether the Stord project and the disputed Stord article are the same, and document which articles make up the 4 pilot articles and 3 pilot projects behind 96 and 44.\"},\"suggested_fix\":\"Align footnote 3 with Table 3 and Section 4.1, and document how the 96 articles and 44 projects were built from the pilot and rounds 2 to 4, including any overlap.\"},{\"id\":\"NUM-003\",\"category\":\"temporal_coverage_inconsistency\",\"finding_summary\":\"The appendix says listings from January to April 2024 are missing, but the Figure 3 discussion says only the first quarter of 2024 is missing, and Figure 3 plots a Q2 2024 point that would rest on May and June only.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"low\",\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"location\":{\"page\":16,\"page_label\":\"16\",\"section\":\"A.2 Figure 3\",\"text_quote\":\"The first quarter of 2024 is missing\",\"precision\":\"exact\"},\"claim_text\":\"The first quarter of 2024 is missing due to the gap in the listing text data. The gap in 2024 is due to missing listing text from January to April 2024.\",\"assessment\":\"partially\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"Appendix A.1 and the Figure 3 caption state that listing text from January to April 2024 (4 months) is missing. The text under Figure 3 says the first quarter of 2024 (3 months) is missing. In the figure image the series ends at Q4 2023 and resumes at the second quarter of 2024, so the Q2 2024 point appears to be plotted although April is missing. If so, that quarter's share rests on two of three months and is not comparable to full-quarter shares. The 2023 to 2024 step across the gap (about 33.6 % to 38.9 % by eye) is read from a figure with no printed values. The headline rise of about 9 percentage points (35 % to 44 %) uses the first and last plotted quarters and is not affected.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 16 text, Figure 3 discussion\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_016.md\",\"page\":16,\"page_label\":\"16\",\"section\":\"A.2\",\"text_quote\":\"The first quarter of 2024 is missing\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-8f4894d007c8a183053537bc\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Series position of the post-gap point was read visually from figures/figure_3.png; no point values are printed.\"},{\"id\":\"S2\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 15 text, Appendix A.1\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_015.md\",\"page\":15,\"page_label\":\"15\",\"section\":\"A.1\",\"text_quote\":\"Listings from January to April 2024 are missing due to a gap in the listing text data.\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-6402cd90a906f1f82672a86f\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Verbatim sentence on page 15.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"Only Q1 2024 is missing\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\",\"S2\"],\"relation\":\"partial\",\"note\":\"Four missing months span all of Q1 and one month of Q2.\"}],\"numeric_check\":{\"kind\":\"cross_source\",\"quantities\":[{\"value\":\"4\",\"source_id\":\"S2\",\"unit\":\"months\",\"sample\":\"FINN.no listings, January 2020 to October 2026\",\"denominator\":\"not applicable (count)\",\"context\":\"Appendix A.1: months with missing listing text (January to April 2024)\",\"rounding_half_width\":\"0.5\",\"scale\":\"number\"},{\"value\":\"1\",\"source_id\":\"S1\",\"unit\":\"quarters\",\"sample\":\"quarterly series in Figure 3\",\"denominator\":\"not applicable (count)\",\"context\":\"Figure 3 discussion: quarters stated as missing (first quarter of 2024)\",\"rounding_half_width\":\"0.5\",\"scale\":\"number\"}],\"comparability\":\"Both statements refer to the same listing-text gap and the same quarterly series, so the 4 missing months should correspond to 1 full quarter plus one month of the next quarter.\",\"observed_discrepancy\":\"4 missing months (January to April) cover Q1 2024 (3 months) plus April, so Q2 2024 is partially missing. The text mentions only Q1 as missing.\",\"possible_explanation\":\"The Q2 2024 point may be computed on May and June only, or the Q2 point may be treated as complete by mistake.\",\"suggested_correction\":\"State how Q2 2024 was computed (May to June only versus excluded). Flag or drop the partial quarter, and describe the missing period consistently as January to April 2024.\"},\"suggested_fix\":\"Describe the gap consistently as January to April 2024 and state whether the Q2 2024 point uses May and June only, or remove that point.\"},{\"id\":\"NUM-004\",\"category\":\"definition_conflict_affecting_shares\",\"finding_summary\":\"Appendix A.3.2 says a word that fits several types gets the first type in Table 9, yet its example and Figure 2's colours place 'bysykkelstativ' and 'syklende' under Infrastructure, although they also contain Parking & storage and Proximity word parts that come earlier. The assignment behind the Table 8 and Figure 2 shares cannot be reproduced from the stated rule.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"low\",\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"location\":{\"page\":18,\"page_label\":\"18\",\"section\":\"A.3.2 Classification of cycling mentions\",\"text_quote\":\"If a word fits more than one type, it is given the first type in the table.\",\"precision\":\"exact\"},\"claim_text\":\"If a word fits more than one type, it is given the first type in the table. For example: words containing “bysykkel” (city bike), such as “bysykkelstativ”, are counted as infrastructure.\",\"assessment\":\"partially\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"Table 9 lists the types in the order: The word 'bicycle', Proximity, Parking & storage, Leisure, Infrastructure, Other. Under the first-type rule, 'bysykkelstativ' contains 'stativ' (a Parking & storage part, listed before Infrastructure), and 'syklende' contains 'sykle' (a Proximity part, listed before Infrastructure). Yet Table 9 lists 'syklende' and 'bysykkel' under Infrastructure, and the appendix text says 'bysykkelstativ' is infrastructure. Figure 2 colours both words blue (Infrastructure). Figure 2 shares are 0.5 % for bysykkelstativ and 0.3 % for syklende, while Table 8 reports Infrastructure at 13.4 % of listings. The category shares in Table 8 (and the 13 % / 11 % statements in Section 6.1 and Appendix A.2) depend on this rule, so a priority-order conflict could shift them. The size of any shift cannot be computed, because word-level listing counts and the overlap with other words are not retained. This is a definition inconsistency only. The paper may have implemented explicit exceptions that are not described.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 18 text, Appendix A.3.2\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_018.md\",\"page\":18,\"page_label\":\"18\",\"section\":\"A.3.2\",\"text_quote\":\"If a word fits more than one type, it is given the first type in the table.\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-03dcf11e0f4853cb90843dbe\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Table 9 type order and word parts read from page 18 text; table_9.md is single-column and was not used for pairing.\"},{\"id\":\"S2\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 16 text, Figure 2 word shares\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_016.md\",\"page\":16,\"page_label\":\"16\",\"section\":\"A.2 Figure 2\",\"text_quote\":\"bysykkelstativ (city bike station) 0.5%\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-8f4894d007c8a183053537bc\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Blue (Infrastructure) bar colouring for bysykkelstativ and syklende was read visually from figures/figure_2.png.\"},{\"id\":\"S3\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 15 text, Table 8 text\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_015.md\",\"page\":15,\"page_label\":\"15\",\"section\":\"A.2 / Table 8\",\"text_quote\":\"In total, 38.1 percent of the listings mention cycling in some way.\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-6402cd90a906f1f82672a86f\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Table 8 rows (Infrastructure 93 786, 13.4 %) are in the same page text.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"Type assignment follows the first matching type in Table 9\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\",\"S2\",\"S3\"],\"relation\":\"partial\",\"note\":\"The stated priority and the stated examples differ for 'bysykkelstativ' and 'syklende'.\"}],\"numeric_check\":{\"kind\":\"underdetermined\",\"question\":\"Would applying the stated first-type rule literally change the Infrastructure, Parking & storage and Proximity shares in Table 8 (13.4 %, 10.8 %, 10.7 %)?\",\"quantities\":[{\"value\":\"0.5\",\"source_id\":\"S2\",\"unit\":\"percent of all listings\",\"sample\":\"700 579 FINN.no listings, January 2020 to October 2026 (excluding January to April 2024)\",\"denominator\":\"all listings\",\"context\":\"Figure 2: share of listings containing 'bysykkelstativ'\",\"rounding_half_width\":\"0.05\",\"scale\":\"percent\"},{\"value\":\"13.4\",\"source_id\":\"S3\",\"unit\":\"percent of all listings\",\"sample\":\"700 579 FINN.no listings\",\"denominator\":\"all listings\",\"context\":\"Table 8: share of listings with an Infrastructure-type mention\",\"rounding_half_width\":\"0.05\",\"scale\":\"percent\"}],\"missing_information\":[\"Word-level listing counts and which words were assigned to which type in the code\",\"Whether explicit exceptions (for example for 'bysykkel' words and 'syklende') override the first-type rule\",\"The overlap between listings containing these words and listings containing other infrastructure words\"],\"status\":\"unresolved\"},\"suggested_fix\":\"State the actual priority order or exceptions used, so the example and Table 9 agree with the rule, and confirm that Table 8's type shares and Figure 2's colours follow that implementation.\"}],\"notes\":[\"No external sources were retrieved. Literature figures (Copenhagen 60 percent trips; up to five percentage points in Bergen; 96 percent accuracy in Bartik et al.) cannot be verified from the parsed artifacts and are recorded as cannot_verify in the coverage ledger.\",\"The 97% agreement figures are arithmetically correct (33/34 = 97.06%) and identical in Tables 3 and 4. They are dominated by 30 of 34 jointly not-relevant articles. A trivial all-not-relevant rule would agree with the pilot coder on 31/34 = 91.2%. The paper already says the comparison is indicative only, so this is not reported as a numerical defect.\",\"Table 5: the sum of the two road-system reference segments (m0–1492 and m0–641) is 2,133 m against a reported length of 2,132 m. This is rounding-compatible and not reported.\",\"Table 7: the text says 'between a third and a half' of projects, but the counts range from 4 of 44 (about 9 percent) to 24 of 44 (about 55 percent). The 'most of the other groups' qualifier keeps this approximate-language point below the materiality threshold.\",\"Structured table CSV/markdown cells were not used for cell assignment, following the parser-quality warnings. Values were read from the page text, with the table PNGs consulted only where the parser audit had compared them. Table 3 and 4 body cells match the page text. Figure 1's chart was read from page_images/page_004.png, and Figures 2 and 3 from their crops.\"],\"completion\":{\"version\":1,\"state\":\"complete\",\"scope_description\":\"Numerical audit of the whole 18-page paper: central pilot, validation, full-run and listing-data claims in full_text.md, all nine tables and four figures, and the appendix definitions, with recomputation of counts, percentages, sums and shares where the denominators were stated.\",\"checks_completed\":[\"Read the parser-quality output and used page text and page images instead of the structured table cells\",\"Recomputed the pilot funnel (70 hits, 36 duplicates, 34 unique, 49%, 15%), the coding split 5+8+21, and 2/34, 5/34 and 1/34 shares\",\"Recomputed Tables 3 and 4 (marginals, 33/34 = 97%) and compared them with the text and the abstract\",\"Checked Table 2 article counts against Section 3.1.4 and Figure 1\",\"Checked Table 6 sums (19+10+9+5 = 43; 19+10 = 29), the 14-of-15 county count, and the 92 and 43 headline counts against the abstract, introduction and conclusion\",\"Checked 96 articles and 44 projects (footnote 3), 18+26 = 44, and 17+2 = 19 day-dated projects against Table 6\",\"Recomputed every Table 8 share against 700 579, the sums 266 625+433 954 = 700 579 and the not-additive note, and compared the Figure 2 word shares with the Table 8 type shares\",\"Read the Figure 3 series from the image, checked the 35 to 44 percent and 9 percentage point claims, and checked the quarter coverage against Appendix A.1\",\"Checked the Figure 4 county values against the text (61/52/38 percent; 14/13/7 percent; 15 counties)\",\"Checked Table 5 (2,132 m vs 1,492+641; one month between 4 Sept and 5 Oct; weekday of 5 Oct 2026)\",\"Checked Table 7 counts against the text and the Table 9 type rule against its examples\"],\"missing_checks\":[],\"evidence_limitations\":[\"Figure 3 has no printed point values; the 35 and 44 percent endpoints and the post-gap point position were read from the image\",\"The composition of the 4 pilot articles and the overlap with rounds 2 to 4 behind 96 articles and 44 projects is not documented in the paper\",\"Word-level counts and the classification code outputs needed to test Table 9 priority effects are not retained\",\"External literature figures could not be verified from parsed artifacts\",\"Table CSV/markdown structured cells were not used, per parser-quality warnings; values came from page text and images\"]},\"coverage\":[{\"item_id\":\"assigned_remit\",\"source_object_ids\":[],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Systematic pass through the numbers in the abstract, body, tables, figures and appendix with recomputation of counts, percentages, sums and shares and comparison of text claims with displays.\",\"status\":\"linked_finding\",\"reason\":\"Four defects were found (NUM-001 to NUM-004). The other checked identities and shares reconciled. The Section 6.2 'between a third and a half' wording was read and judged too approximate to report.\",\"finding_ids\":[\"NUM-001\",\"NUM-002\",\"NUM-003\",\"NUM-004\"],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"display:OBJ-0b391e5449c5ec2c409159ce\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-0b391e5449c5ec2c409159ce\"],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Figure 1 chart values (70, 34 with 49 %, 5 with 15 %, 2) read from the page 4 image and compared with the surrounding text and Section 3.1.4.\",\"status\":\"linked_finding\",\"reason\":\"The percentages are correct (34/70 = 48.6 %, 5/34 = 14.7 %), but the text and caption share '2 of 34 (about 6 percent)' and '2 of 70' mix openings with articles and hits.\",\"finding_ids\":[\"NUM-001\"],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"display:OBJ-95ecd7ea3c5b8d724f48fffa\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-95ecd7ea3c5b8d724f48fffa\"],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Compared Figure 2 word shares with the text (9.8 % for sykkel; around 5 % for the next four words) and with the Table 8 type shares, including union-versus-sum bounds per type.\",\"status\":\"linked_finding\",\"reason\":\"The shares match the text and Table 8 (sykkel 9.8 % = 68 974 / 700 579). The category colouring of bysykkelstativ and syklende conflicts with the stated first-type rule (NUM-004).\",\"finding_ids\":[\"NUM-004\"],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"display:OBJ-7414d59aee2e3ad29a820798\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-7414d59aee2e3ad29a820798\"],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Read the Figure 3 series from the image and compared the endpoints (about 35 % to about 44 %, so 9 percentage points), the dip in the second half of 2023, and the gap with Appendix A.1.\",\"status\":\"linked_finding\",\"reason\":\"The 9 percentage point rise and the 2023 dip match the image. The gap description (Q1 only versus January to April) is inconsistent.\",\"finding_ids\":[\"NUM-003\"],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"peripheral\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"display:OBJ-d491066348ee1a73c8bce8f5\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-d491066348ee1a73c8bce8f5\"],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Compared the Figure 4 county shares in the page 17 text with the text claims (Oslo 61, Akershus 52, Norway 38; Telemark 14, Nordland 13, Møre og Romsdal 7) and counted 15 counties.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"All stated county figures match the bars (60.8, 52.0, 14.4, 12.9, 6.8). The three lowest counties are correctly identified (Troms 15.5 is higher). The dashed Norway line equals the 38.1 % in Table 8, though its numeric label was not read from the image.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"peripheral\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"display:OBJ-8ec9459ce44d02c62d0a27bf\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-8ec9459ce44d02c62d0a27bf\"],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Read Table 1 from the page 5 text and checked its numeric content (3 km, 3.5 m, 4 articles in 2 newspapers) against Table 2 and Table 7.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"Table 1 is mostly descriptive. The Homborsund article count (4) matches Table 2, and the date resolution (Monday = 5 October 2026) is correct.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"display:OBJ-dbc6f4a9a830bbbd128e371e\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-dbc6f4a9a830bbbd128e371e\"],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Read Table 2 from the page 5 text and reconciled the article counts (4+1+1+1) and dates with Section 3.1.4, Figure 1 and the footnotes.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"The 5 relevant articles (4 Homborsund + 1 Stod) and the 2 planned-opening articles agree with Figure 1 and Section 3.1.4. The structured CSV was not used because it is truncated.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"display:OBJ-e3774f186493fe1cf3afe556\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-e3774f186493fe1cf3afe556\"],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Checked the Table 3 marginals (3+0, 1+30; 3+1, 0+30; total 34), 33/34 = 97 %, and the consistency with Section 4.1 and footnote 3.\",\"status\":\"linked_finding\",\"reason\":\"The table is internally consistent, but the Stord article's coding by the second coder contradicts footnote 3 (NUM-002).\",\"finding_ids\":[\"NUM-002\"],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"display:OBJ-a9068469a80185aaa27470fa\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-a9068469a80185aaa27470fa\"],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Checked the Table 4 marginals and agreement (33/34 = 97 %), the three-run majority rule, and the 1 of 34 (3 %) internal-consistency claim in Section 3.2.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"Table 4 is internally consistent and equal to Table 3. 1/34 = 2.9 % rounds to 3 %. The only 'disagreement' is the Stord article.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"display:OBJ-e6d7a21f6dac33af8ecba464\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-e6d7a21f6dac33af8ecba464\"],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Read Table 5 from the page 9 text and checked length against the segment ranges, one month between the NVDB registration date and the opening, municipality and county numbers, and elevation and climb plausibility.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"Segment lengths 1 492 m + 641 m = 2 133 m against the reported 2,132 m, which is rounding-compatible. 4 September to 5 October 2026 is about one month.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"display:OBJ-ff90f28affccf4abf5c4e78c\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-ff90f28affccf4abf5c4e78c\"],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Checked Table 6 sums (19+10+9+5 = 43; 19+10 = 29), 14 of 15 counties, and the 92, 43 and 50-outlet figures against the abstract, Section 5 and the conclusion.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"Totals reconcile and the headline numbers are consistent. Panel values were read from the page text, not the interleaved markdown. Day-dated projects 19 = 17+2 from Section 6.3.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"display:OBJ-2c51ead4a14e19e7c3de50a9\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-2c51ead4a14e19e7c3de50a9\"],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Read the Table 7 counts from the page 11 text, computed shares of 44 projects, and checked them against the Section 6.2 narrative.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"42 of 44 (95 %) is 'almost all'. The 'between a third and a half' wording is approximate (4 of 44 for delay reasons; 24 of 44 for project status) but is qualified by 'most', so it was not reported. The 44 projects relate to footnote 3, which is covered in NUM-002.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"display:OBJ-8cd1361a5f77a70c5008ee4d\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-8cd1361a5f77a70c5008ee4d\"],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Recomputed every Table 8 share from the page 15 text counts over 700 579, checked 266 625+433 954 = 700 579, and compared the per-type shares with the text and Figure 2.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"All shares are correct to one decimal. The type rows sum to 346 827 listings against 266 625 with any mention, as the note explains. Structured markdown splits thousands, so the page text was used.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"display:OBJ-b0c2f2010110ab92e8c28a1c\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-b0c2f2010110ab92e8c28a1c\"],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Read Table 9 and Appendix A.3.2 from the page 18 text and tested the first-type priority rule against the examples and the Figure 2 colours.\",\"status\":\"linked_finding\",\"reason\":\"The type priority stated in the text conflicts with the example and with the assignment of some words (NUM-004).\",\"finding_ids\":[\"NUM-004\"],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"claim:literature-magnitudes\",\"source_object_ids\":[],\"claim\":\"Copenhagen bicycle lane network increased bicycle trips by 60 percent; Bergen tunnel increased cycling shares by up to five percentage points; Bartik et al. reach 96 percent accuracy.\",\"check_performed\":\"Looked for supporting numbers in the retained artifacts; the paper reports only the cited figures.\",\"status\":\"cannot_verify\",\"reason\":\"The cited publications are not part of the parsed artifacts and no external retrieval was performed in this role, so the values and their percent versus percentage-point units cannot be confirmed.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"peripheral\",\"scope\":\"sampled\"}]}","structured_output":{"reviewer":"numerical_auditor","paper_id":"bicycle-infrastructure-paper","run_status":"ok","summary":"Most reported counts, percentages and shares reconcile. These include the 70/36/34 pilot funnel, 49% and 15% retained, the 5+8+21 coding split, Tables 3 and 4, Table 6's date-precision sum to 43, 18+26=44 and 17+2=19 day-dated projects, Table 8's 266,625+433,954=700,579 and every share, and the figure and text values for Figures 2 to 4. Four material numerical defects remain. (1) The headline pilot yield of \"2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent)\" divides distinct openings by articles. The article-level count is 5 coded relevant (15%) or 3 after recoding, and Figure 1's caption makes the same openings-over-hits mix. (2) Footnote 3 says the Stord article was coded not relevant by both human coders, but Table 3 and the Section 4.1 text imply the second coder coded it relevant. (3) The Q1 2024 gap in Figure 3 is described as a missing quarter, while the appendix says January to April 2024 is missing. (4) The Table 9 first-type rule contradicts its own example, which affects the category shares in Table 8 and Figure 2. Parser-quality warnings were respected: figures and tables were checked against page text and images, and the structured table CSV/markdown was not used for cell assignment.","findings":[{"id":"NUM-001","category":"unit_denominator_mismatch","finding_summary":"The pilot yield \"Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place\" divides 2 distinct openings by 34 articles. At article level the paper itself reports 5 articles coded relevant (15 % of 34), or 3 after recoding.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"medium","confidence":"high","location":{"page":4,"page_label":"4","section":"3.1.2 Pilot sample / Figure 1","text_quote":"Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place.","precision":"exact"},"claim_text":"Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place. Figure 1 caption: Only 2 of 70 search hits concern a cycling infrastructure opening that actually took place.","assessment":"partially","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"The numerator '2' is the count of distinct openings (Homborsund and Stod/Steinkjer), and the denominator is articles (34) or search hits (70). Figure 1 shows 5 unique articles coded relevant (15 % retained) and 2 distinct openings. Section 3.1.4 says there are five relevant articles but only two unique openings (four on Homborsund, one on Stod), and that two Homborsund articles were published before the opening, leaving three relevant articles. Table 2 also lists 4 articles for Homborsund. 2/34 = 5.9 %, which matches the printed 'about 6 percent' arithmetically, but the share of articles concerning a realised opening is 5/34 = 14.7 % (Figure 1's 15 %), or 3/34 = 8.8 % under the validation recoding. The statement therefore mixes units (events over articles), and it understates the article-level yield. The abstract's 'only two actual openings' counts events and is not affected. The same openings-over-hits mix appears in the Figure 1 caption ('2 of 70 search hits'). The caption's own 70 hits include duplicates, so the number of hits tied to these two openings is not reported.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"page 4 text (Section 3.1.2 and Figure 1)","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_004.md","page":4,"page_label":"4","section":"3.1.2","text_quote":"Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place.","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-1a119a3cb70f2153fc56dd97","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Figure 1 chart values (70; 34, 49 % retained; 5, 15 % retained; 2) confirmed on page_images/page_004.png, since the figure_1 crop omits the chart."},{"id":"S2","type":"text","label":"page 4 text, Figure 1 labels","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_004.md","page":4,"page_label":"4","section":"Figure 1","text_quote":"Coded “relevant” 5 15 % retained","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-1a119a3cb70f2153fc56dd97","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Page text matches the chart seen in the page image."},{"id":"S3","type":"text","label":"full_text.md, Section 3.1.4","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/full_text.md","page":5,"page_label":"5","section":"3.1.4 Manual coding of the pilot","text_quote":"Of the five relevant articles, only two unique openings were found.","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-a50e96cda8f251a4eabe3e71","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Section 3.1.4 sits on page 5, and the registered object covers the whole full_text.md."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"2 of 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place","source_object_ids":["S1","S2","S3"],"relation":"partial","note":"2 is a count of openings, not of articles. The article-level counts in the paper are 5 (15 %) or 3."}],"numeric_check":{"kind":"cross_source","quantities":[{"value":"6","source_id":"S1","unit":"percent","sample":"34 unique pilot articles","denominator":"34 unique articles","context":"printed share, numerator is 2 distinct openings, not articles","rounding_half_width":"0.5","scale":"percent"},{"value":"15","source_id":"S2","unit":"percent","sample":"34 unique pilot articles","denominator":"34 unique articles","context":"Figure 1: share of unique articles coded 'relevant' (5 of 34 = 14.7 %)","rounding_half_width":"0.5","scale":"percent"},{"value":"2","source_id":"S3","unit":"distinct openings","sample":"5 articles coded relevant in the pilot","denominator":"not applicable (count)","context":"Section 3.1.4: five relevant articles describe only two unique openings","rounding_half_width":"0.5","scale":"number"}],"comparability":"The 6 % and the 15 % both use the 34 unique pilot articles as the denominator, but the numerators count different units: 2 distinct openings versus 5 articles coded relevant (3 after the pre-opening Homborsund articles are recoded). They are not the same estimand, and the 6 % is labelled as an article share.","observed_discrepancy":"The article-level share that the paper itself reports is 5/34 = 14.7 % (Figure 1 shows 15 %), or 3/34 = 8.8 % after recoding. The text reports 2/34 = 5.9 % as an article share.","possible_explanation":"The authors may have meant the share of unique openings per article, or may have intended to say 2 openings from 34 articles. The numerator unit was not carried through.","suggested_correction":"State the unit explicitly. Either report the article-level yield (5 of 34, 15 %, or 3 of 34 after recoding) or phrase it as 'the 34 articles describe only 2 distinct openings'. Apply the same to the Figure 1 caption's '2 of 70 search hits'."},"suggested_fix":"Rewrite the sentence and the Figure 1 caption so the numerator and denominator use the same unit (articles with articles, or events with events), and reconcile the 6 % with the 15 % shown in Figure 1."},{"id":"NUM-002","category":"cross_source_count_inconsistency","finding_summary":"Footnote 3 says the Stord project was coded not relevant by both human coders, but Table 3 and Section 4.1 show the second coder coded the one disputed article, the Stord article, as relevant.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"medium","confidence":"medium","location":{"page":10,"page_label":"10","section":"6 Further Uses of the Articles, footnote 3","text_quote":"the project in Stord, which appears only in the pilot and was coded as not","precision":"exact"},"claim_text":"The 44 projects include the 43 in Section 5 and the project in Stord, which appears only in the pilot and was coded as not relevant by both human coders (Section 4).","assessment":"no","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"Table 3 gives the pilot coder Relevant = 3 and the second coder Relevant = 4. The only off-diagonal cell is pilot Not relevant / second Relevant = 1, so the second coder coded one extra article as relevant. Section 4.1 says the single disagreement 'covers a dispute between Stord municipality and Vestland County', and Section 4.2 says the same Stord article is the only pilot coder versus LLM disagreement. Footnote 3 instead states that both human coders coded the Stord project as not relevant. Both statements cannot hold for the same article. The pilot-article tally supports the Table 3 and 4 reading: the footnote's 'four from the pilot run' (96 = 92 + 4) matches the 4 LLM-relevant articles in Table 4 (the 3 agreed articles plus Stord). If the Stord project and the disputed Stord article are distinct, the paper does not say so. The composition of the 4 pilot articles and the overlap of the pilot with rounds 2 to 4 are not documented, so the 96 and 44 totals cannot be rebuilt from retained evidence.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"page 10 text, footnote 3","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_010.md","page":10,"page_label":"10","section":"6 footnote 3","text_quote":"the project in Stord, which appears only in the pilot and was coded as not","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-e9b90e0f75ce05ff1ce150cc","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Footnote text is split across lines in the page text; the quoted fragment is verbatim."},{"id":"S2","type":"text","label":"page 7 text, Section 4.1","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_007.md","page":7,"page_label":"7","section":"4.1","text_quote":"The only article they disagree on covers a dispute between Stord municipality and Vestland County over a pedestrian crossing.","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-4e6da301f188ae5c7e5d0a63","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Table 3 cells (3, 0, 3; 1, 30, 31; 4, 30, 34) agree between the page text and the parser-quality audit's comparison with the table image."},{"id":"S3","type":"table","label":"Table 3 markdown (body cells)","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/tables/table_3.md","page":7,"page_label":"7","section":"Table 3","text_quote":"Overall agreement 97% (33 of 34 articles)","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-e3774f186493fe1cf3afe556","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Header cells in the markdown are merged, so the second-coder total of 4 was read from page 7 text and the table image."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"Stord project coded not relevant by both human coders","source_object_ids":["S1","S2","S3"],"relation":"contradicts","note":"Table 3 shows the second coder with 4 relevant versus 3 for the pilot coder, and the disputed article is the Stord article."}],"numeric_check":{"kind":"cross_source","quantities":[{"value":"4","source_id":"S3","unit":"articles","sample":"34 unique pilot articles","denominator":"not applicable (count)","context":"Table 3: second coder 'Relevant' total","rounding_half_width":"0.5","scale":"number"},{"value":"3","source_id":"S3","unit":"articles","sample":"34 unique pilot articles","denominator":"not applicable (count)","context":"Table 3: pilot coder 'Relevant' total","rounding_half_width":"0.5","scale":"number"},{"value":"1","source_id":"S2","unit":"articles","sample":"34 unique pilot articles","denominator":"not applicable (count)","context":"the only article on which the two human coders disagree is the Stord article","rounding_half_width":"0.5","scale":"number"}],"comparability":"The same 34 pilot articles and the same relevance coding are used in Table 3, Section 4.1 and footnote 3. The footnote's 'project in Stord' may refer to a different article from the disputed 'Stord municipality and Vestland County' article, which the paper does not clarify.","observed_discrepancy":"If the Stord project is the disputed article, the second coder coded it relevant (Table 3 total 4 vs 3, one off-diagonal article), which contradicts footnote 3's 'not relevant by both human coders'.","possible_explanation":"The footnote may refer to a different Stord project article, or may describe an earlier coding version.","suggested_correction":"Reconcile footnote 3 with Table 3 and Section 4.1, state explicitly whether the Stord project and the disputed Stord article are the same, and document which articles make up the 4 pilot articles and 3 pilot projects behind 96 and 44."},"suggested_fix":"Align footnote 3 with Table 3 and Section 4.1, and document how the 96 articles and 44 projects were built from the pilot and rounds 2 to 4, including any overlap."},{"id":"NUM-003","category":"temporal_coverage_inconsistency","finding_summary":"The appendix says listings from January to April 2024 are missing, but the Figure 3 discussion says only the first quarter of 2024 is missing, and Figure 3 plots a Q2 2024 point that would rest on May and June only.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"low","confidence":"medium","location":{"page":16,"page_label":"16","section":"A.2 Figure 3","text_quote":"The first quarter of 2024 is missing","precision":"exact"},"claim_text":"The first quarter of 2024 is missing due to the gap in the listing text data. The gap in 2024 is due to missing listing text from January to April 2024.","assessment":"partially","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"Appendix A.1 and the Figure 3 caption state that listing text from January to April 2024 (4 months) is missing. The text under Figure 3 says the first quarter of 2024 (3 months) is missing. In the figure image the series ends at Q4 2023 and resumes at the second quarter of 2024, so the Q2 2024 point appears to be plotted although April is missing. If so, that quarter's share rests on two of three months and is not comparable to full-quarter shares. The 2023 to 2024 step across the gap (about 33.6 % to 38.9 % by eye) is read from a figure with no printed values. The headline rise of about 9 percentage points (35 % to 44 %) uses the first and last plotted quarters and is not affected.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"page 16 text, Figure 3 discussion","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_016.md","page":16,"page_label":"16","section":"A.2","text_quote":"The first quarter of 2024 is missing","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-8f4894d007c8a183053537bc","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Series position of the post-gap point was read visually from figures/figure_3.png; no point values are printed."},{"id":"S2","type":"text","label":"page 15 text, Appendix A.1","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_015.md","page":15,"page_label":"15","section":"A.1","text_quote":"Listings from January to April 2024 are missing due to a gap in the listing text data.","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-6402cd90a906f1f82672a86f","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Verbatim sentence on page 15."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"Only Q1 2024 is missing","source_object_ids":["S1","S2"],"relation":"partial","note":"Four missing months span all of Q1 and one month of Q2."}],"numeric_check":{"kind":"cross_source","quantities":[{"value":"4","source_id":"S2","unit":"months","sample":"FINN.no listings, January 2020 to October 2026","denominator":"not applicable (count)","context":"Appendix A.1: months with missing listing text (January to April 2024)","rounding_half_width":"0.5","scale":"number"},{"value":"1","source_id":"S1","unit":"quarters","sample":"quarterly series in Figure 3","denominator":"not applicable (count)","context":"Figure 3 discussion: quarters stated as missing (first quarter of 2024)","rounding_half_width":"0.5","scale":"number"}],"comparability":"Both statements refer to the same listing-text gap and the same quarterly series, so the 4 missing months should correspond to 1 full quarter plus one month of the next quarter.","observed_discrepancy":"4 missing months (January to April) cover Q1 2024 (3 months) plus April, so Q2 2024 is partially missing. The text mentions only Q1 as missing.","possible_explanation":"The Q2 2024 point may be computed on May and June only, or the Q2 point may be treated as complete by mistake.","suggested_correction":"State how Q2 2024 was computed (May to June only versus excluded). Flag or drop the partial quarter, and describe the missing period consistently as January to April 2024."},"suggested_fix":"Describe the gap consistently as January to April 2024 and state whether the Q2 2024 point uses May and June only, or remove that point."},{"id":"NUM-004","category":"definition_conflict_affecting_shares","finding_summary":"Appendix A.3.2 says a word that fits several types gets the first type in Table 9, yet its example and Figure 2's colours place 'bysykkelstativ' and 'syklende' under Infrastructure, although they also contain Parking & storage and Proximity word parts that come earlier. The assignment behind the Table 8 and Figure 2 shares cannot be reproduced from the stated rule.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"low","confidence":"medium","location":{"page":18,"page_label":"18","section":"A.3.2 Classification of cycling mentions","text_quote":"If a word fits more than one type, it is given the first type in the table.","precision":"exact"},"claim_text":"If a word fits more than one type, it is given the first type in the table. For example: words containing “bysykkel” (city bike), such as “bysykkelstativ”, are counted as infrastructure.","assessment":"partially","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"Table 9 lists the types in the order: The word 'bicycle', Proximity, Parking & storage, Leisure, Infrastructure, Other. Under the first-type rule, 'bysykkelstativ' contains 'stativ' (a Parking & storage part, listed before Infrastructure), and 'syklende' contains 'sykle' (a Proximity part, listed before Infrastructure). Yet Table 9 lists 'syklende' and 'bysykkel' under Infrastructure, and the appendix text says 'bysykkelstativ' is infrastructure. Figure 2 colours both words blue (Infrastructure). Figure 2 shares are 0.5 % for bysykkelstativ and 0.3 % for syklende, while Table 8 reports Infrastructure at 13.4 % of listings. The category shares in Table 8 (and the 13 % / 11 % statements in Section 6.1 and Appendix A.2) depend on this rule, so a priority-order conflict could shift them. The size of any shift cannot be computed, because word-level listing counts and the overlap with other words are not retained. This is a definition inconsistency only. The paper may have implemented explicit exceptions that are not described.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"page 18 text, Appendix A.3.2","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_018.md","page":18,"page_label":"18","section":"A.3.2","text_quote":"If a word fits more than one type, it is given the first type in the table.","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-03dcf11e0f4853cb90843dbe","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Table 9 type order and word parts read from page 18 text; table_9.md is single-column and was not used for pairing."},{"id":"S2","type":"text","label":"page 16 text, Figure 2 word shares","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_016.md","page":16,"page_label":"16","section":"A.2 Figure 2","text_quote":"bysykkelstativ (city bike station) 0.5%","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-8f4894d007c8a183053537bc","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Blue (Infrastructure) bar colouring for bysykkelstativ and syklende was read visually from figures/figure_2.png."},{"id":"S3","type":"text","label":"page 15 text, Table 8 text","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_015.md","page":15,"page_label":"15","section":"A.2 / Table 8","text_quote":"In total, 38.1 percent of the listings mention cycling in some way.","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-6402cd90a906f1f82672a86f","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Table 8 rows (Infrastructure 93 786, 13.4 %) are in the same page text."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_tex